<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  These are declared ~1.2x larger than the size they render at on One UI, and at exactly
  the size they render at everywhere else. Both halves of that are now measured:

    Samsung One UI (Galaxy S23, density 480)
      Renders third-party widget RemoteViews at density 400 — a 0.833 factor, confirmed
      across several declared sizes and a freshly added instance, and not caused by
      targetCellWidth. 67dp declared measured 56dp on screen.

    Pixel Launcher (emulator, API 36, density 480)
      No rescale at all. 67dp declared measured 201px = 67dp on screen, and the glyph
      measured 31dp against the 25dp it renders at on One UI.

  So a single dp value cannot be right on both, and the 1.2x is kept because it targets
  the device this was tuned and approved on. The cost is that the pill is about a fifth
  larger on Pixel-family launchers — larger, not broken, and it still reads as a pill.

  Fixing it properly means sizing in pixels the app computes itself, which needs
  RemoteViews.setViewLayoutHeight (API 31+) for the bar; setViewPadding already takes
  pixels at any API level. That is tracked in the roadmap rather than done here, because
  it only pays off once there is a second device to keep honest.

  Declared -> rendered on One UI (the tuned target):
    bar height    67dp -> 56dp   (taller than the stock 48dp pill, by preference)
    glyph         30dp -> 25dp   (same as the stock Google G)
-->
<resources>
    <dimen name="widget_bar_height">67dp</dimen>
    <!-- (67 - 30) / 2, keeping the glyph proportional as the pill grows. -->
    <dimen name="widget_slot_padding">18dp</dimen>
    <!-- Breathing room at the pill's ends, so the outer glyphs are not flush to the edge. -->
    <dimen name="widget_bar_padding">10dp</dimen>
</resources>
